# The one place a tool version is pinned. Nothing else declares one.
#
# Consumers, all of which read this file rather than restating it:
#
#   scripts/install-dependencies.sh   sources it, to provision a workstation
#   .github/runner-image/Dockerfile   takes them as build arguments
#   .github/workflows/runner-image.yml sources it to pass those arguments
#   .github/actions/versions          exports them into a workflow job
#
# They have to agree. A plan produced by one version of tofu and applied by
# another is the kind of difference nobody sees until it matters, and
# "works on my machine" is the whole failure mode a self-hosted runner
# invites. That was not hypothetical: CI validated pull requests on OpenTofu
# 1.10.6 for as long as this file said 1.12.6, because the workflow declared
# its own copy.
#
# tests/go/repo/versions_test.go asserts that no consumer restates a key.
#
# Its companion is approved-suppliers.yml beside this file: this one pins WHAT
# VERSION of a thing the estate takes, that one declares WHO it may be taken
# from. Two files rather than one only because this must stay KEY=VALUE for the
# shell consumers above to source it.
#
# Keys are alphabetical, and nothing enforces that any more. dotenv-linter did,
# failing the build on UnorderedKey, and it arrived inside the aggregate that
# went in #365 - so the ordering is now a convention rather than a rule. It is
# kept because a hand-scanned list of thirty pins is easier to search sorted,
# not because anything depends on it: no consumer reads this file in order.
# Grouping by purpose would read better and was not worth a red check while
# there was one to avoid; it is available now, to whoever decides it is worth
# the diff.
#
# Decisions only, never a checksum. A hash is not a decision - it is a fact
# about what a supplier served for a version somebody decided on - so it lives
# in scripts/deliveries.lock, written by `task order-deliveries` from the
# versions here. HADOLINT_SHA256 and its siblings sat beside their versions
# until #416, which meant a hash could be edited by hand and nothing asked
# whether it was ever the file's. Node's checksums are the exception still:
# per-architecture, and in the one script that downloads it.
#
# A model id is a version pin, and belongs here for the same reason a tool
# version does. `gemini-flash-latest` and its siblings are published alongside
# the dated ids and are mutable pointers - `:latest` in a different spelling,
# where nothing in git records that it moved - so only exact ids are pinned
# here. The API confirms the pin held: a generateContent response echoes back
# `modelVersion`, and it must equal what was asked for rather than whatever an
# alias resolved to that day.
#
# The keys are named for the role rather than the vendor, matching the vault
# field `llm_key` and the `independent-review` supplier group. Changing which
# party answers is then these lines plus one endpoint, not a rename.
ANSIBLE_CORE_VERSION=2.21.3
CHECKOV_VERSION=3.3.17
CILIUM_VERSION=1.20.1
CLERK_MODEL_VERSION=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
CLERK_SWEEP_MODEL_VERSION=gemini-3.8-flash
CODESPELL_VERSION=2.4.3
FLUX_VERSION=2.7.1
GO_VERSION=1.26.7
HADOLINT_VERSION=2.15.1
HELM_VERSION=4.3.0
INSPECTOR_MODEL_VERSION=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
KUBECONFORM_VERSION=0.7.0
KUBECTL_VERSION=v1.36.4
NODE_VERSION=24.19.0
PRE_COMMIT_HOOKS_VERSION=4.6.0
PRE_COMMIT_VERSION=4.6.2
RCLONE_VERSION=1.75.0
SHELLCHECK_VERSION=0.11.0
RUNNER_VERSION=2.337.0
TALOSCTL_VERSION=v1.13.8
TASK_VERSION=3.53.1
TOFU_VERSION=1.12.6
TRUFFLEHOG_VERSION=3.97.1
ZIZMOR_VERSION=1.30.1
